@kind(writ)

#[ Writ: workspace root ]

Rules the whole repository obeys.

Scope is this directory and everything under it. A narrower rule lives in a
`.writ.tmt` closer to what it governs; read every `.writ.tmt` from a file up
to here, the way `.gitignore` is read.

Only the author edits this file. An agent that finds a rule in its way
proposes an edit here and stops. It does not work around the rule, and it
does not relax the rule to fit the change it wanted to make.

##[ crate-layering ]

Every workspace member belongs to exactly one layer, and depends only on
layers strictly below its own. Same-layer dependencies are allowed;
upward ones are not.
/tests/SYNTAX.md
@layers{
  6: [ "apps/*", "bindings/*", "editors/*", "tests" ]
  5: [ "crates/tomet-workspace", "crates/tomet-workspace-indexer", "crates/tomet-workspace-links" ]
  4: [ "crates/tomet-format*", "crates/tomet-convert-*", "crates/tomet-transform", "crates/serde_tomet" ]
  3: [ "crates/tomet-config" ]
  2: [ "crates/tomet-semantics*" ]
  1: [ "crates/tomet-syntax-*" ]
  0: [ "crates/tree-sitter-tomet" ]
}

Layer 0 depends on no workspace member at all. Things may depend on it --
`tests` and `apps/tui` both do -- but it may depend on nothing, because it
is the hand-maintained grammar approximation and is only worth having while
it stays independent of the parser.

`crates/serde_tomet` is listed by name in layer 4. It is an ecosystem
adapter rather than a producer, and its name carries no layer prefix to
match on.

A pattern ending in `*` matches by prefix; every other pattern is exact.
Patterns are directory paths relative to the workspace root, because the
directory is the half of a crate's identity that is arranged by layer --
`crates/tomet-syntax-parser` publishes `tomet-parser`.

Dev-dependencies are exempt. A crate may reach upward in `[dev-dependencies]`
to get a parser for its own tests -- `tomet-syntax-tree` and
`tomet-semantics` both do, and neither is a layering violation.

---[ Why ]---

The order is what keeps a change local. Touching the parser today means
reading `crates/tomet-syntax-*` and nothing else; the moment an edge runs
upward, it means reading the applications too.

The layer is derived from the directory name, not the package name. The two
disagree -- `crates/tomet-syntax-parser` publishes `tomet-parser` -- and the
directory is the half that is arranged by layer, which is why the workspace
member list in `Cargo.toml` is already grouped this way in comments.

Every member's directory now agrees with its layer. It did not always:
`crates/tomet-config` was `crates/tomet-workspace-config`, which read as
part of the workspace layer while the format and convert crates were
sitting on top of it. The rule carried it as a named exception until the
rename, rather than quietly filing it under the wrong prefix -- an
exception a reader can see is worth more than a tidy table that lies.

The rejected alternative is a rule per crate. It states the same thing and
cannot answer the question that matters -- whether some crate has no rule at
all. Deriving the layer from the directory prefix means a new member that
matches no prefix and no exception line above is unclassified, and an
unclassified member is a failure rather than a silence.

---[ Guard ]---

`twrit check`. It reads `@layers` above, reads every workspace member's
`[dependencies]` through `cargo_metadata`, and fails on a member matching
no layer, a member matching two, an upward edge, or anything `crates/
tree-sitter-tomet` comes to depend on.

##[ blueprint-shape ]

A blueprint declares what it is, what it targets, and what it says about
itself -- in that order, in the three slots every element has.

```tmt
@kind(blueprint)
@blueprint(writ){
  version: "1.0.0"
  description: "twrit"
}
```

`@kind(blueprint)` is written, never inferred from `@blueprint` being
present. The target is the positional argument, normalized to `target`.
`@blueprint`'s `{...}` holds the blueprint's own metadata and never the
target. Instantiating promotes `@blueprint(X)` to `@kind(X)`.

---[ Why ]---

`why/root.tmt` puts it plainly: identifier, input, data, and a setting that
says where each one goes. A blueprint has all three and nothing else --
what it is, what it is parameterised by, what it records about itself. The
shape is not invented for blueprints; it is the shape, applied.

`@kind` is the one answer to "what is this document". Inferring it from an
element's presence makes a second path to the same question, and a second
path is what has gone wrong every time: `@meta{type:}` beside `@kind`, the
identifier rules differing between element arguments and interpolation, `-`
and `#` taking different groups from `@name`.

Rejected, and worth naming because the code accepted all of them at once:

- `@blueprint(kind: writ)`. `@kind(blueprint)` sits two lines above, so
  `kind` would appear twice in three lines meaning two different things --
  this file's kind, and the output's.
- `@blueprint(target: writ)`. Spelling the key is not wrong, only
  redundant: `embed` and `link` already normalize a positional to `target`,
  and `@kind(writ)` is positional too, so promotion becomes the same value
  moving rather than a change of shape.
- `@target(writ)` as a separate element. It makes the promotion easy to
  see, but the target is a fact *about the blueprint* and belongs with its
  other facts. One element fewer.
- A document counting as a blueprint because it has any `@kind` at all.
  That is what `extract_blueprint_schema` did, and it is why nothing could
  tell a blueprint from the documents it describes.

None of this was written down when `@blueprint` was implemented in
`bb38aff`, and it appears nowhere in `docs/spec/`. That is the whole reason
this entry exists.

---[ Guard ]---

None yet. Two halves are checkable: no document carries `@blueprint`
without `@kind(blueprint)`, and `@blueprint`'s args are a bare positional
rather than a `target:`/`kind:` map.

##[ kind-not-meta-type ]

A document declares its kind with a top-level `@kind(...)`. The `type:`
field inside `@meta` is the superseded spelling and does not appear in this
repository -- not in documentation, not in examples, not in fixtures.

---[ Why ]---

A kind selects the schema that validates the document, so it is a directive
about the document, not a field of its metadata. Buried inside `@meta` it
was reachable only through a nested lookup, and a reader had to open a map
to find out what they were holding.

This rule exists because the decision had already been made and tooled --
`promote_meta_type_to_kind`, and a `refactor` subcommand to run it -- and
still lost ground. With no rule naming it, `docs/guide/` went on teaching
the old spelling, `docs/spec/builtin-settings.tmt` was itself written in it,
and eleven files under `docs/` had drifted back by the time anyone counted.
The migration existed; the rule did not.

---[ Guard ]---

`tomet refactor --check --meta-kind` -- non-zero while any file still
carries the old spelling.

Known gap: files that fail to parse are skipped with a warning rather than
failing the run, so a broken file is also an unguarded one.
